\section{Limitations}\label{sec:limitations}

\paragraph{Model-specific caveats.}
R1-32B achieves significance only at $W{=}1$ (25\% coverage, 5/20 traces); dimensionality ($51200 \times W$ for $n{=}20$) prevents larger windows, and joint (layer, $W$) grid search was not conducted for R1-32B ($n{=}20$ is the fundamental bottleneck). R1-32B's HS advantage is position-mediated: the residualized HS-text gap is non-significant (+1.9pp, $p{=}0.914$; \cref{app:position_residual}). QwQ-32B's position baseline achieves higher balanced accuracy (0.832 vs.\ HS 0.796) and HS lead time is negative. R1-8B FPR significance is seed-fragile (3/5 HB). Robustness varies by model (OT-7B 5/5 HB, $\Delta$Prec std${=}1.3$pp; R1-8B 3/5 HB, std${=}4.5$pp), not by scale.

\paragraph{High false positive rate and oracle gap.}
Safe-trace FPR is 39--52\% at standard thresholds, so current probes characterize the phenomenon rather than constitute production detectors. The 85.5\% oracle gap (computed on R1-8B only; \cref{sec:oracle}) confirms that passive probing captures only a fraction of available signal.

\paragraph{Post-hoc power at 32B.}
At $n{=}19$--$20$, the minimum detectable effect at 80\% power is 8.6--9.5pp (\cref{app:power_analysis}). R1-32B's residualized HS-text gap (+1.9pp) falls well below this threshold, so non-significance is consistent with either a true null or insufficient power. All 32B conclusions marked $\dagger$ should be interpreted with this constraint.

\paragraph{Cumulative hyperparameter selection.}
Layer (up to 64), $W$ (7 values), $K$ (5 values), and threshold are selected on the same validation set ($n{=}4$--$9$ per-class validation traces at 32B scale), yielding up to $64 \times 7 \times 5 = 2{,}240$ configurations. $C$-insensitivity (${<}2$pp) covers one hyperparameter; the cumulative researcher degrees of freedom are substantial, particularly at 32B where per-fold sample sizes are small. $K$ was set as a design pre-selection (\cref{app:k_ablation}), and the $K \times W$ joint surface is not fully explored. Joint (layer, $W$) stability was verified via grid search for QwQ-32B ($W \in \{1,3,5\}$; \cref{app:layer_sweep}), but full leave-one-fold-out validation of the (layer, $W$) pair and extension to R1-32B remain for future work. Bootstrap CIs at $n{=}19$--$20$ may exhibit below-nominal coverage~\citep{efron1993bootstrap}; $p$-value resolution with 10{,}000 resamples is $10^{-4}$.

\paragraph{CP annotation uncertainty.}
CP annotations follow the HarmThoughts methodology~\citep{harmthoughts}, which defines commitment points through content analysis of reasoning steps. We do not independently evaluate inter-annotator agreement (IAA). If CP annotation error is ${\geq}2$ steps, the reported lead times (+4.5 to +13 steps) may partially fall within annotation noise, particularly at the lower end of this range; QwQ-32B's lead time ($-$1.2 to +1.9 steps) falls entirely within such noise. Future work should quantify annotation reliability and assess its impact on probe evaluation.

\paragraph{Scope and data representativeness.}
We cover 2 architectures and 2 scales (7--8B, 32B); intermediate scales (13B, 70B) and proprietary reasoning models (o1, o3) are missing. The text baseline uses MiniLM-384d; BGE-large-1024d narrows the gap (1/4 HB-significant). All results use HarmThoughts, a single English-language benchmark with a specific harm taxonomy and annotation protocol; generalization to multi-turn, multilingual, encoded-prompt settings, or alternative harm taxonomies is untested. HarmThoughts traces were generated under specific prompting conditions, and the distribution of commitment point positions may not reflect naturally occurring self-jailbreak patterns. Probing results are correlational; causal intervention is needed to confirm mechanistic involvement.

\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Layer selection as researcher degree of freedom.}
Layer selection is an underexamined methodological choice that can silently determine a study's conclusions. Our five-metric ablation (\cref{sec:layer-results}) demonstrates that crossing rate reverses the central finding: at biased layers, the HS advantage appears scale-dependent; at corrected layers, all four models are significant$^\dagger$ (\cref{sec:evaluation}). The mechanism is representation compression at shallow layers (\cref{app:cosine_volatility}), which inflates crossing rate independently of discriminative power. We hypothesize the effect generalizes beyond safety probing to any threshold-based detection study. The HS advantage is robust across BA-optimal (mid-depth) and precision-optimal (deep) layers; it is the crossing-rate bias toward shallow layers that masks the effect (\cref{tab:ba_layer_comparison}: all 16 model-metric comparisons pass HB at BA-selected layers, precision +13.7 to +25.4pp). This strengthens our recommendation: any threshold-independent layer selection criterion recovers the HS advantage.

\paragraph{Temporal context and modality choice.}
Both temporal context and modality choice improve detection, but their relative magnitudes differ by scale: at 7--8B, the modality advantage exceeds temporal improvement (\cref{sec:temporal}); at 32B, relative magnitudes are model-dependent. Temporal windowing ($W{=}3$ to $15$) provides consistent improvement regardless of modality, while modality choice requires principled layer selection.

\paragraph{Encoder-conditional advantage.}
Part of the observed advantage may reflect text encoder quality. At matched 384d via PCA (79--92\% variance retained), the precision advantage persists on all four models$^\dagger$ (+16.8 to +23.1pp, HB corrected), eliminating capacity gap as the sole explanation. A stronger 1024d encoder closes most of the significance gap (1/4 HB-significant), suggesting a continuum rather than a binary modality distinction. LLM-based text encoders (e.g., E5-Mistral-7B, 4096d; \citealp{wang2024e5mistral}) would likely further narrow the gap, bounding the practical lifetime of any HS advantage claim.

\paragraph{Per-model evidence and design implications.}
Robustness splits by model, not scale: OT-7B and QwQ-32B (5/5 seeds HB each) are most stable, R1-8B FPR is seed-fragile (3/5 HB), and R1-32B passes all seeds but its advantage is position-mediated (residualized gap NS; \cref{app:position_residual}). Distillation and native reasoning training produce qualitatively different safety representation geometry within the same architecture. PCA capacity control, control task selectivity, and position residualization (3/4 models retain significant non-positional HS advantage) provide converging evidence of genuine safety representations; direct erasure methods (LEACE; \citealp{belrose2023leace}) remain untested.

